# Everything this service currently knows about every endpoint, as RDF rather
# than as rows. This is the machine-readable representation of the index
# resource (see web/app.py); web/queries/index.rq answers the same question as
# SELECT rows for the HTML representation.
#
# This is web/queries/endpoint_description.rq with the ?endpoint substitution
# taken out, minus its class-sample branch, and the two files have to move
# together for the reason that one gives: the index and the endpoint page are
# two views of one set of facts, and a resource whose representations selected
# their runs differently would have two notions of "newest".
#
# Why a CONSTRUCT and not the SELECT beside it, in one sentence rather than the
# five endpoint_description.rq spends on it: every triple in the template below
# also appears, verbatim, in some run graph in this store, so this document
# cannot assert anything the graph does not already say. Assembling it in Python
# from web/queries/index.rq's bindings would make the two representations two
# independent re-derivations of the same facts, and the RDF one would be free to
# state a verdict some code chose to write rather than one the prober published.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why it is a smaller document than the union of 543
# endpoint descriptions:
#
#   * No class sample. endpoint_description.rq's third branch reads
#     the sample pointers and emits each sample's values, its size, its
#     truncation flag and its own activity. The index draws no sample and says
#     nothing about one, so emitting several hundred sw:sampledValue triples per
#     endpoint would publish, in the index's name, facts no row of the index
#     stands behind. An endpoint's own resource is where its sample is served,
#     and every endpoint here is named, so a consumer that wants one knows where
#     to ask.
#   * No triple about the index itself. There is no sw:Index resource, no
#     rdfs:member list and no count. Every one of those would be a triple no run
#     graph holds, invented here to describe the document rather than the
#     endpoints, and this query's whole guarantee is that it invents nothing. The
#     membership of the index IS the set of endpoints that appear as a
#     dqv:computedOn or sw:notMeasuredOn object below, which is exactly the set
#     the HTML lists.
#
# THE DORMANCY PATTERN IS A BRANCH OF THE NEWEST-ACTIVITY ARM'S OWN UNION, and
# not a fourth OPTIONAL in that arm's group nor a fourth top-level arm. Both of
# those were written, measured and rejected, and one of them was shipped for a
# commit. The store the numbers are about is the DEPLOYED shape, which no
# committed fixture has: 543 endpoints, an older sweep that measured all of
# them, and a newest sweep that completes 486 and declares the other 57 dormant,
# 77,209 quads.
#
# Timed interleaved in one process, best of fifteen, so the columns are
# comparable to each other. The absolute level moves with what else this machine
# is doing (an earlier session measured the first three at 99, 491 and 94 ms);
# the multiplier is what reproduces, and it is the claim:
#
#                                          min      vs baseline   triples
#   no dormancy branch at all            320 ms         1.0x       26,615
#   an OPTIONAL in the same group      1,927 ms         6.0x       26,786
#   its own fourth top-level arm         331 ms         1.0x       26,786
#   a branch of this arm's UNION         282 ms         0.9x       26,786
#
# The last three emit exactly the same 26,786 triples, verified triple for
# triple over this store and over 28 stores built from the committed fixtures.
# So the only question the three shapes answer differently is cost, and one of
# them also answers a question about what stays pinned:
#
#   * THE SAME-GROUP OPTIONAL shares with that group only variables that are
#     constant within it, so nothing joins and the group's solutions become its
#     completion markers TIMES its dormancy declarations: 486 x 57 = 27,702
#     solutions where the facts wanted are 543. Six times the cost for 171 extra
#     triples. This repository rejects shapes on exactly this basis;
#     web/queries/index.rq refuses an 1,832 ms one and
#     web/queries/endpoint_description.rq a 2,183 ms one.
#   * THE FOURTH TOP-LEVEL ARM is correct and as fast, and it was what this file
#     shipped for one commit. What it costs is a SECOND copy of the newest-run
#     aggregate, and a copy is a place the selection can drift. Mutating MAX to
#     MIN one copy at a time: the fourth arm's copy reddened
#     test_the_index_turtle_carries_it_too and the original arm's copy reddened
#     NOTHING, so which run supplied sw:emission, sw:finalised and
#     sw:completedEndpoint to this document was pinned by no test at all.
#     Splitting the arms handed the dormancy test the new copy and left the
#     older one bare.
#   * THE NESTED UNION keeps one copy, which cannot drift from itself, and the
#     same mutation reddens that test again. That is why it is the shape here
#     rather than a third test over the fourth arm.
#
# The aggregate's own cost is 0.4 ms at 402 runs and 1.0 ms at 1002, measured in
# web/queries/endpoint_measurements.rq beside its own copy of it.
#
# web/queries/endpoint_description.rq and web/queries/index.rq needed none of
# this: both scope the dormancy pattern to a single ?endpoint, substituted in the
# first and bound per row in the second, so it multiplies nothing there. This
# file is the one place the pattern is asked of every endpoint at once, which is
# what makes the cross product available at all.
#
# Measured on this machine over the 543-endpoint registry sweep: 26,103 triples
# serialised to about 2.2 MB of Turtle in 130 ms. The byte count moves by a few
# tens of KB between runs while the triple count does not, because a CONSTRUCT's
# solution order is not specified and the Turtle writer collapses adjacent
# triples that share a subject. That is a bulk representation and it is meant to
# be one; the HTML representation of the same resource is the one with a size
# budget.

PREFIX dqv: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dqv#>
PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>
PREFIX sw: <urn:sparqlwatch:>

CONSTRUCT {
  ?measurement a ?measurementType ;
               dqv:computedOn ?endpoint ;
               dqv:isMeasurementOf ?metric ;
               dqv:value ?verdict ;
               prov:wasGeneratedBy ?activity ;
               sw:level ?level ;
               sw:elapsedMs ?elapsedMs .
  ?notMeasured a ?notMeasuredType ;
               sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint ;
               sw:notMeasuredMetric ?declinedMetric ;
               sw:notMeasuredReason ?reason ;
               prov:wasGeneratedBy ?declineActivity .
  # Whether the run a row's facts came from said how it was written, and whether
  # it said it finished. Written against both activity variables because exactly
  # one of them is bound per solution, and an unbound subject drops its triple.
  ?activity a prov:Activity ;
            prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt ;
            sw:emission ?emission ;
            sw:finalised ?finalised ;
            sw:completedEndpoint ?completedEndpoint .
  ?declineActivity a prov:Activity ;
                   prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt ;
                   sw:emission ?emission ;
                   sw:finalised ?finalised ;
                   sw:completedEndpoint ?completedEndpoint .
  # The newest activity in the store, with the three run-level facts stage
  # 1c-b4 writes. What is emitted is the INPUTS to "a newer run stopped before
  # it reached this endpoint" and never that conclusion: both conclusions rest
  # partly on an absence, a CONSTRUCT emits only presence, so a derived flag
  # could be built for the HTML representation and not for this one and the two
  # would disagree about exactly the run this branch exists for.
  ?newestActivity a prov:Activity ;
                  prov:generatedAtTime ?newestGeneratedAt ;
                  sw:emission ?newestEmission ;
                  sw:finalised ?newestFinalised ;
                  sw:completedEndpoint ?newestCompleted ;
                  sw:dormantEndpoint ?newestDormant .
  # The declaration's own two facts. Their subject is ?newestDormant, which is
  # bound only in the arm below, so they drop everywhere else. See that arm on
  # why the variable is a fresh one rather than ?endpoint.
  ?newestDormant sw:dormancyReason ?newestDormancyReason ;
                 sw:dormantSince ?newestDormantSince .
}
WHERE {
  {
    GRAPH sw:current {
      # The pointer inside each branch, and the type pattern as a bound variable
      # plus a FILTER. Both shapes are measured, in web/queries/index.rq's
      # header and in endpoint_measurements.rq's.
      ?endpoint sw:currentRun ?run .
      ?measurement dqv:computedOn ?endpoint ;
                   dqv:isMeasurementOf ?metric ;
                   dqv:value ?verdict ;
                   prov:wasGeneratedBy ?activity ;
                   a ?measurementType .
      FILTER (?measurementType = dqv:QualityMeasurement)
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:level ?level }
      OPTIONAL { ?measurement sw:elapsedMs ?elapsedMs }
    }
    OPTIONAL {
      GRAPH ?run {
        ?activity a prov:Activity ;
                  prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
        OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:emission ?emission }
        OPTIONAL { ?activity sw:finalised ?finalised }
      }
    }
    FILTER (BOUND(?generatedAt))
    # The completion marker for THIS row's endpoint, in its own OPTIONAL rather
    # than nested inside the branch above. See web/queries/index.rq's note on
    # the same split: an OPTIONAL group is left-joined on the variables it
    # shares, so a group that binds ?endpoint narrows to the endpoints the run
    # completed, and nesting it here would take the run's timestamp down with it
    # and drop the whole solution at the FILTER above. On this query that would
    # silently omit an endpoint from the document rather than mis-state a fact
    # about it, which is harder to notice and no better.
    OPTIONAL {
      GRAPH ?run { ?activity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint }
      BIND (?endpoint AS ?completedEndpoint)
    }
  }
  UNION
  {
    GRAPH sw:current {
      ?endpoint sw:currentRun ?declineRun .
      ?notMeasured sw:notMeasuredOn ?endpoint ;
                   sw:notMeasuredMetric ?declinedMetric ;
                   sw:notMeasuredReason ?reason ;
                   prov:wasGeneratedBy ?declineActivity ;
                   a ?notMeasuredType .
      FILTER (?notMeasuredType = sw:NotMeasured)
    }
    OPTIONAL {
      GRAPH ?declineRun {
        ?declineActivity a prov:Activity ;
                         prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
        OPTIONAL { ?declineActivity sw:emission ?emission }
        OPTIONAL { ?declineActivity sw:finalised ?finalised }
      }
    }
    FILTER (BOUND(?generatedAt))
    # The completion marker for THIS row's endpoint, in its own OPTIONAL rather
    # than nested inside the branch above. See web/queries/index.rq's note on
    # the same split: an OPTIONAL group is left-joined on the variables it
    # shares, so a group that binds ?endpoint narrows to the endpoints the run
    # completed, and nesting it here would take the run's timestamp down with it
    # and drop the whole solution at the FILTER above. On this query that would
    # silently omit an endpoint from the document rather than mis-state a fact
    # about it, which is harder to notice and no better.
    OPTIONAL {
      GRAPH ?declineRun { ?declineActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?endpoint }
      BIND (?endpoint AS ?completedEndpoint)
    }
  }
  UNION
  {
    # THE NEWEST ACTIVITY IN THE STORE: its three run-level facts, the endpoints
    # it recorded finishing, and the endpoints it declared dormant. ONE arm, with
    # the aggregate and the activity match hoisted above a NESTED UNION that
    # separates the last two lists.
    #
    # THE SHAPE IS MEASURED AND THIS FILE'S HEADER HOLDS THE NUMBERS. In short:
    # the dormancy pattern as a fourth OPTIONAL in the group below costs six
    # times this because the group's solutions become its completion markers
    # TIMES its dormancy declarations, and as a fourth top-level arm it costs
    # nothing but needs a second copy of the aggregate, which is a place the
    # selection of "newest" can drift and which was measurably pinned by no test.
    #
    # The aggregate subquery is the same shape as web/queries/index.rq's and
    # web/queries/endpoint_measurements.rq's, because the three have to agree
    # about which run is newest or the representations of one resource disagree
    # about which run a fact came from.
    {
      SELECT (MAX(?anyGeneratedAt) AS ?newestGeneratedAt) WHERE {
        GRAPH ?anyRun {
          ?anyActivity a prov:Activity ;
                       prov:generatedAtTime ?anyGeneratedAt .
        }
      }
    }
    # Hoisted, so that both branches below are about the same activity and the
    # aggregate is asked once. Its prov:generatedAtTime is what makes everything
    # below dateable, the dormancy declarations included: a dormant endpoint's
    # verdicts come from an OLDER run, so this document holds two activities, and
    # a declaration that named neither would invite a consumer to date it to the
    # sweep that took the measurements rather than to the sweep that took none.
    GRAPH ?newestRun {
      ?newestActivity a prov:Activity ;
                      prov:generatedAtTime ?newestGeneratedAt .
    }
    {
      # What that run said about itself, and which endpoints it recorded
      # finishing. All three OPTIONAL, because a run captured before stage
      # 1c-b4 carries none of them and its facts are still the answer.
      #
      # Its sw:completedEndpoint is emitted for EVERY endpoint it names here,
      # unlike in endpoint_description.rq where it is scoped to the one endpoint
      # that document describes. That is not a widening of what is claimed: this
      # document describes every endpoint in the index, so every completion
      # marker of that run is a fact about a resource it describes.
      GRAPH ?newestRun {
        OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:emission ?newestEmission }
        OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:finalised ?newestFinalised }
        OPTIONAL { ?newestActivity sw:completedEndpoint ?newestCompleted }
      }
    }
    UNION
    {
      # Which endpoints that run declared dormant, with the reason and the
      # instant it published for each. A SEPARATE BRANCH of this arm's UNION
      # and not a fourth OPTIONAL beside the three above: that is the 27,702
      # solutions named at the top of this arm.
      #
      # A FRESH VARIABLE, mirroring ?newestCompleted above, and NOT ?endpoint.
      # The other two arms of the outer UNION use that name for the endpoint a
      # measurement or a decline is about, so binding it here would give one
      # name two meanings across the arms of one query.
      #
      # KEYED ON sw:currentRun, which is what makes "a resource it describes"
      # true of a dormancy. An endpoint the newest run ONLY declared dormant has
      # no measurement and no decline, so no row of web/queries/index.rq
      # mentions it and the index does not list it. Unkeyed, this branch would
      # carry a dormancy for a resource the index omits, and both
      # representations of this one resource have to describe the same set of
      # endpoints. See the comment above web/app.py's knownness test for why the
      # omission is right and is not widened here.
      #
      # Only the newest run's declaration is read, for the reason
      # web/queries/index.rq gives at the same point.
      GRAPH ?newestRun {
        ?newestActivity sw:dormantEndpoint ?newestDormant .
        OPTIONAL { ?newestDormant sw:dormancyReason ?newestDormancyReason }
        OPTIONAL { ?newestDormant sw:dormantSince ?newestDormantSince }
      }
      GRAPH sw:current { ?newestDormant sw:currentRun ?dormantEndpointIsListed }
    }
  }
}
